  "response": "I verified all three findings against the fixture source rather than taking the scan output at face value. All three are true positives — the evidence lines match the files exactly.\n\n## Verdicts\n\n| # | Finding | Verdict | Why |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| crime_00030 | `docs_code_drift` — `docs/teams.md:5 → ./setup.md` | **Block merge** (scoped) | Objectively true, zero-judgement, one-line fix |\n| crime_00005 | `duplicated_navigation_source` — two nav registries | **Warn** | Real structural defect, but the fix is a design decision |\n| crime_00006 | `route_metadata_drift` — `/settings/billing` | **Warn** (fold into 00005) | Same incident as 00005; not an independent gate |\n\n## crime_00030 — docs_code_drift → block\n\nLowest severity of the three (0.35) but the strongest block candidate, and the ranking-by-severity intuition gets this backwards. `docs/teams.md:5` links `[setup guide](./setup.md)`; `docs/` contains only `billing.md`, `commands.md`, `teams.md`. That's a filesystem fact with no interpretation layer — confidence 0.90 is honest, and there is no plausible false-positive story. An agent following that doc hits a dead end and, in my experience, invents the setup steps rather than stopping.\n\nScope the gate: block when the diff touches `docs/` or the referenced path, warn otherwise. A pre-existing broken link shouldn't fail a PR that never went near documentation.\n\nOne thing the finding can't tell you: whether `setup.md` was deleted or never existed. That decides the fix (restore vs. remove the link), and it's a `git log` away, not a scanner question.\n\n## crime_00005 — duplicated_navigation_source → warn\n\nThis is the actual root cause of the pair. `src/nav/registry.ts` and `src/nav/sidebar.ts` both own `/settings/billing`, with different shapes (`{path, title}` vs `{to, label}`) and different labels (`Plans` vs `Billing`). Two writable sources for one destination means every future nav edit is a coin flip on which file the editor finds first.\n\nNot a block, because the fix — pick a canonical source, derive the other — is a refactor with a design decision in it, and the scanner offers no basis for choosing. Worth adding: **neither nav file is imported anywhere in the repo.** Nothing consumes `navRegistry` or `sidebar`, so \"which one is live\" is undecidable from the code. The detector doesn't guess, which is right, but it means a reviewer can't resolve this from the finding alone — they need the product owner. That's a warn with a name attached, not a gate.\n\n## crime_00006 — route_metadata_drift → warn, folded into 00005\n\nVerified in `src/routes/settings/billing.tsx`: `metadata.title = \"Plans\"`, `<title>Subscription</title>`, component `PricingPage`, path/file `billing`. Four vocabularies for one destination, exactly as reported.\n\nThe important triage point: **00006 and 00005 are one incident, not two.** 00006's evidence list literally contains 00005's two nav labels. If you gate on both you double-count a single naming decision, and fixing 00005 shrinks 00006 to a three-way disagreement automatically. Treat 00005 as the ticket and 00006 as its acceptance criterion.\n\nConfidence 0.65 is the lowest in this report and appropriately so — \"these strings should agree\" is a heuristic, and there are legitimate designs where the URL, the component name, and the browser tab title differ on purpose. That's exactly the profile you warn on, never block.\n\nOne piece of evidence the detector missed that tips the decision: the page renders `<h1>Choose a plan</h1>`. That's the only user-visible label in the file, and it agrees with `metadata.title = \"Plans\"` and the registry entry. So \"Plans\" is the canonical name with 3 of 5 surfaces already behind it — rename `PricingPage` → `PlansPage` and `<title>` → `Plans`, leave the `/settings/billing` URL alone (URLs are a compatibility surface; renaming costs redirects for no user benefit).\n\n## Context worth carrying into the review\n\nThe same IA cluster produced `concept_alias_drift` (00019 — `account`/`organisation`/`team`/`workspace` across 5 files) and three `orphaned_destination` findings (00034–00036) whose route files are `account/`, `team/`, and `workspace/` — the same four aliases. Those aren't in scope here, but they're the same underlying condition at repo scale: this codebase has no owner for its vocabulary. Fixing the billing route's four names without that is treating one symptom.",
